[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66206,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cowden syndrome, also known as <em>PTEN</em> hamartoma tumour syndrome, is a heterogeneous group of disorders with autosomal dominant inheritance, caused by germline mutation of the <em>PTEN</em> gene and characterized by multiple hamartomas and a predisposition to cancer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66207,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "<h5><strong>MIM numbering</strong></h5>\r\n<p>158350 Cowden syndrome&nbsp;1; CWS1</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66208,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LD2D.Y Other specified phakomatoses or hamartoneoplastic syndromes&nbsp;&ndash; Cowden syndrome</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66209,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> <em>PTEN</em> hamartoma tumour syndrome</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66210,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Bannayan&ndash;Riley&ndash;Ruvalcaba syndrome; Proteus syndrome; Proteus-like syndrome</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66211,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cowden syndrome affects various organs, including the uterus, skin, breast, gastrointestinal tract, brain, thyroid, kidney, and soft tissue.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66212,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Cowden syndrome is difficult to recognize because of its diverse clinical features, which are summarized in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/465/15205\" target=\"_blank\">#15205</a>Box&nbsp;<a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/465/15205\" target=\"_blank\">#15205</a> {10923032; 11496368; 24136893; 22252256}. Affected individuals usually have macrocephaly, dermal trichilemmomas, and papillomatous papules. Gynaecological manifestations include endometrial carcinomas and uterine leiomyomas. Cases that fulfil some but not all diagnostic criteria are classified as Cowden-like syndrome.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-10T14:08:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66213,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>The prevalence is about 1&nbsp;case per 200&nbsp;000&nbsp;population {20301661; 11238682}. Affected individuals usually present by late in the third decade of life. Estimates of the frequency of de&nbsp;novo <em>PTEN</em> mutations range from 10% to 48% {22595938}. The lifetime risk of endometrial carcinoma is about 13&ndash;19% for patients with Cowden syndrome (vs 2.5% in the general population) {26161390}. Patients with endometrial carcinoma are usually 30&ndash;50&nbsp;years of age, but sometimes &lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;years {25376524; 18445858; 23279635; 24778394}. The frequency of Cowden syndrome is very low among patients with apparently sporadic endometrial cancer; in one study, a single pathogenic <em>PTEN</em> germline mutation was found in only 1&nbsp;of 381&nbsp;consecutive cases {27443514}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T12:55:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66214,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cowden and Cowden-like syndromes are caused by germline mutations in <em>PTEN</em> (85% of Cowden syndrome cases) or other genes (SDH genes, <em>PIK3CA</em>, <em>AKT1</em>, or <em>SEC23B</em>) or by germline <em>KLLN</em> promoter hypermethylation {25376524; 21979946}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66215,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PTEN, which is a virtually ubiquitously expressed tumour suppressor, is a dual-specificity lipid and protein phosphatase that regulates cell proliferation, cell migration, and apoptosis through inhibition of AKT via the PI3K/AKT pathway {23613428}. Inactivation of the second copy of the gene allows deregulation of the AKT pathway.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66216,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is nonspecific.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66217,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Uterine carcinomas are usually of low-grade endometrioid type, but serous endometrial carcinomas may occasionally occur {25376524}. Uterine leiomyomas are indistinctive.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66218,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66219,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cowden syndrome is caused primarily by germline <em>PTEN</em> mutations. Other genes are rarely involved: SDH genes, <em>PIK3CA</em>, <em>AKT1</em>, <em>SEC23B</em>, and <em>KLLN</em> {23246288}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66220,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "<p>The International Cowden Consortium (ICC) operational diagnostic criteria are listed in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/465/15205\" target=\"_blank\">#15205</a>Box&nbsp;<a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/465/15205\" target=\"_blank\">#15205</a>.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-10T14:08:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66221,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66222,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 465,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>For endometrial cancer and other malignant tumours, prognosis depends on histological type, grade, and stage. Uterine leiomyomas are benign.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-23T16:17:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]